<Layout>
 <!-- Hero Section -->
    <section class="hero">
      <canvas class="hero-rain" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
      <div class="container hero-content">
        <p class="hero-eyebrow">Frontend Developer</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title" aria-label="Hello, I'm Zohaib">Hello, I'm
          <span class="name-swap" tabindex="0" data-alt="Frontend Developer"><span class="name-swap-text highlight"
              aria-hidden="true">Zohaib</span></span>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-subtitle">
          I care about the details users notice, the performance they feel, and the simplicity they remember. I build
          for the experience, not just the screen.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-buttons">
          <a href="#projects" class="btn-pill">View Projects <span class="arrows"
              aria-hidden="true">&gt;&gt;&gt;</span></a>
          <button type="button" class="btn-pill btn-pill-ghost open-contact-modal">Contact Me</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <!-- The Problem Section -->
    <section id="problem" class="section problem reveal">
      <div class="container">
        <header class="pb-head">
          <p class="section-eyebrow">page.problem</p>
          <h2 class="pb-title">The Problem</h2>
          <p class="pb-lead">Most frontend problems don't start as big mistakes. They start as small decisions nobody
            questions.</p>
        </header>

        <ol class="pb-list">
          <li class="pb-item">
            <span class="pb-num" aria-hidden="true">[01]</span>
            <h3>It works. But does it work everywhere?</h3>
            <p>A website can look perfect on your screen and still fail on someone else's. If it only works where you
              tested it, is it really finished?</p>
          </li>
          <li class="pb-item">
            <span class="pb-num" aria-hidden="true">[02]</span>
            <h3>Why does a small change break something else?</h3>
            <p>If fixing one thing creates another problem, the issue might not be the change. It might be how
              everything is connected.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="pb-item">
            <span class="pb-num" aria-hidden="true">[03]</span>
            <h3>If users have to think too much, what went wrong?</h3>
            <p>A good interface shouldn't make people guess what to click, where to go, or what happens next. The design
              should make the next step obvious.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="pb-item">
            <span class="pb-num" aria-hidden="true">[04]</span>
            <h3>What's the point of a beautiful website that feels slow?</h3>
            <p>Users don't experience your design file. They experience every delay, every jump, and every frustrating
              interaction.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="pb-real">
          <p class="pb-real-label">The Real Issue</p>
          <h3>The problem isn't always what you see. It's what you overlooked.</h3>
          <p>Good frontend development means questioning the small decisions before they become big problems. That's
            where better experiences begin.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- About Section -->
    <section id="about" class="section about reveal">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="ab-box">
          <div class="ab-copy">
            <p class="section-eyebrow">page.about</p>
            <h2 class="ab-title">About</h2>
            <p class="ab-text">
              I'm a frontend developer who cares about the details users notice. I build fast, accessible
              interfaces and ship them to production, from the first sketch to the deployed site. I'm still
              learning every day, and I like it that way.
            </p>
            <a href="#about" class="ab-btn">More about me<span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
          </div>
          <div class="reach-badge ab-badge">
            <span class="cta-status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            Available for freelance projects &middot; Remote / worldwide
          </div>
          <div class="ab-stats">
            <div class="ab-stat">
              <strong class="ab-value"><span data-count="2">2</span><i>+</i></strong>
              <span class="ab-label">live projects</span>
              <small>e-commerce + analytics dashboard</small>

            </div>

            <div class="ab-stat">
              <strong class="ab-value"><span data-count="2">2</span><i>h</i></strong>
              <span class="ab-label">reply time</span>
              <small>usually under 2 hours</small>
            </div>
          </div>
          <!-- My story (shows only on the About page opened from the nav) -->
          <div class="ab-story">
            <div class="ab-story-text">
              <h2 class="ab-story-title">My story</h2>

              <p>I’m a <strong>Frontend Web Developer</strong> in the making who turns logic into clean, usable design.
                I’m a 4th-semester computerscience student, and the way I learn is simple: I build things.</p>

              <p>I wrote my first line of code at 19 on freeCodeCamp, and for a while it was just exercises and syntax.
                Then I decided to build something for a real person. A friend’s birthday was coming up, so instead of
                sending a text, I built him a small website. When he opened it, he went quiet for a second, then grinned
                and said, “You made this? For me?” That moment changed how I saw code. It stopped being homework and
                became a way to make someone’s day.</p>

              <p>That project also taught me my first real lesson: reading about code and shipping it are two very
                different skills. The page looked perfect on my laptop and fell apart on his phone. One CSS rule
                overrode
                another, an image broke the layout, and a button did nothing because of a typo in a JavaScript variable
                name. I spent hours chasing each bug, and I learned more from that one afternoon than from a week of
                tutorials.</p>

              <p>Today I’m working toward becoming a full-stack developer. My mindset is <strong>logic first, design
                  second</strong>, before I write code, I ask what problem I’m solving and how the pieces connect. I’d
                rather understand <em>why</em> something works than copy something that happens to work. If I can’t
                explain a piece of code in my own words, I’m not done with it yet.</p>

              <p>Debugging is also where I found something I didn’t expect: I stay calm when things break. When some
                classmates were building an Adidas shoe store website, bugs kept piling up and nobody could figure out
                why. More than once I heard, “We can’t do this,” and they were ready to give up. I sat down with them,
                read through their code line by line, and helped trace each problem back to its cause. Each time a bug
                disappeared, their energy came back. I realized that debugging isn’t just a technical skill; it’s about
                keeping people’s confidence alive while the code is broken.</p>

              <p>What I care about most is <strong>clarity</strong>, clear code, clear layouts, and clear communication.
                I’d much rather build something small that works well than something massive that falls apart.</p>

              <p>I’m now looking for <strong>internships and remote opportunities</strong> for three reasons. I love
                building new things and learning by shipping them. I enjoy solving people’s problems and giving them a
                design that feels easy to use. And I want to grow into someone who can work independently and build a
                career on work I’m proud of. Away from the keyboard, I’m usually out with friends, or back at my
                computer
                turning small ideas into code. Both keep me grounded, and I bring the same attitude to each: try it, see
                what happens, and learn from it.</p>

            </div>

